  "final_report": "I reviewed Scout\u2019s saved report (`artifact_0960809b-14ee-4465-9ea9-e7d7d53cef37`) against the approved organization, architecture, and delegated-creation documents. Its three risks are well supported and correctly prioritize control-plane trust over model behavior:\n\n1. **Authority leakage.** A worker could treat a message as an assignment or appear to grant a child capabilities it does not possess. Keep creation, messaging, assignment, and execution separate; enforce the delegation ceiling and active execution scope in trusted code. Test attempted escalation through worker creation and messages.\n\n2. **Duplicate or stranded work after failure.** A crash during a Git or integration side effect can leave the outcome uncertain. Retain transactional execution claims and durable intent records; reconcile only demonstrably safe queued work. Block ambiguous attempts for inspection rather than replaying them automatically.\n\n3. **Evidence-poor handoffs.** A plausible summary could conceal an unmet requirement or a review of the wrong revision. Require explicit acceptance criteria, attributable artifacts, exact-commit review, and full tests on the integration candidate before reporting delivery complete.\n\n**Next step:** make these three failure modes release gates in a bounded local validation run: escalation-denial tests, interruption/restart recovery tests, and exact-commit review-to-integration checks. Scout\u2019s report is a document-based assessment, not fresh runtime validation; it does not establish that the current deployment passes those gates.",
